On 3 Dec 2006 02:20:23 -0800, "Simon" <yaroguelike@googlemail.com>
wrote:

> I can completely understand their current position.  The DevTeam all
> have jobs in real life, and developing Nethack will be one of a number
> of demands on each member's free time.
> 
> Any public announcements of features would lead to endless debate /
> comments / suggestions on rgrn which, if they get involved in the
> debate, would slow down the implementation of these features.
> 
> Any public announcement of the next release date will simply put
> unnecessary time pressure on them - much better to simply let things
> take as long as they take so that you don't have to worry about missing
> artificial deadlines.


There are other approaches they could take which would not put undue
burden on their time.  "Announce and ignore" - keep the community at
least updated on general timelines and new features, but ignore or
limit their involvement in followup requests and argumentation.
Immersion in r.g.r.n drama is not a requirement.  Many developers for
internet communities - even for software more popular than nethack -
take this more balanced approach.

I think much better than this, though, would be opening the
development process to a public wiki, keeping some part of the devteam
(or other volunteers) in a coordinator and "overall game direction
vision" role.  Too much duplicated and dead-end effort goes on with
all the patches, and the overall development process is stagnant - I
don't care what marvels are finally revealed in the next release, 3
years between releases, and hundreds of "resolved" bugs (some of them
critical to gameplay) rotting in a queue on a web page, are ridiculous
for such a popular game with such an active community.  There are many
talented programmers and players with excellent ideas and time to
implement them, which could be bringing us fun new releases more
efficiently and more quickly.

Jim


